//@version=6
// ============================================================================
// Breaking Ray [vganesh]
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Anchors a horizontal ray to a single, user-selected candle (its top,
// bottom, or both). The ray extends indefinitely to the right until price
// breaks it — at that point it freezes into a fixed line segment running
// from the anchor candle to the candle that broke it.
//
// Usage
//   - Add the indicator; TradingView will prompt you to click the candle
//     you want to anchor to (same click-to-place mechanic as the built-in
//     "Anchored VWAP").
//   - Set "Anchor Candle's Timeframe" to whatever timeframe you were
//     viewing when you clicked (e.g. "D" if you clicked on a Daily chart).
//     This is required for the ray to show the correct price if you ever
//     view the chart on a different timeframe afterward — see 3.0.0 below.
//   - One instance == one anchor candle. To track several candles, add
//     several instances of this indicator, each anchored to a different one.
//   - To reposition an anchor later, open Settings and click the target
//     icon next to "Anchor Candle" — no need to re-add the indicator.
//
// Version: 3.7.1
// Changelog:
//   3.7.1 - Branded all on-chart and in-code mentions of "Breaking Ray" as
//           "Breaking Ray [vganesh]" — indicator title, header comment, and
//           the warning/nudge label text.
//   3.7.0 - Added the standard TradingView open-source publish header
//           (Mozilla Public License 2.0 + © vganesh) ahead of publishing.
//   3.6.1 - Clarified that alert() and alertcondition() are two separate
//           trigger paths in TradingView's "Create Alert" dialog: picking
//           condition "Ray Broken" fires the fixed alertcondition() message
//           (can't carry which ray broke or the price — it's just a single
//           yes/no flag), while "Any alert() function call" fires the
//           dynamic "{{ticker}} Top/Bottom ray broken at <price>" message
//           added in 3.6.0. Added {{ticker}} to the alertcondition()
//           fallback message too, for consistency.
//   3.6.0 - Alert messages now read "{{ticker}} Top ray broken at <price>"
//           / "{{ticker}} Bottom ray broken at <price>". {{ticker}} is a
//           real TradingView placeholder — substituted with the actual
//           symbol when the alert fires — kept literal in the string on
//           purpose so one alert works cleanly across multiple charts.
//   3.5.0 - Changed default "Price Label Position" to Left (was Right).
//           Added "Show Price Label After Break" (default off): a broken
//           ray now drops its price label by default instead of persisting
//           it, matching the pre-3.4.0 behavior; turn the new option on to
//           keep showing it. The label section is now fully state-driven —
//           every bar it either shows the label at the correct spot or
//           ensures it's removed, rather than special-casing the break
//           transition in the break-check block.
//   3.4.0 - Added "Price Label Position" (Left/Center/Right). Right matches
//           the old behavior (follows the ray's current right edge — the
//           latest bar while active, the breaking bar once broken), which
//           is why it kept drifting right on lower timeframes as more bars
//           formed. Left pins it to the fixed anchor bar; Center sits at
//           the midpoint and freezes once the ray breaks. Also: the label
//           now persists after a break (frozen in place) instead of
//           disappearing, unless "Keep Broken Rays As Lines" is off, in
//           which case there's no ray left to label.
//   3.3.0 - Changed where the ray starts, by user preference: previously it
//           started at the beginning of the anchor candle (its Daily open,
//           say), which on a lower timeframe could sit several bars before
//           the specific candle that actually printed the high/low. Now
//           locates the exact lower-timeframe bar where the extreme
//           actually occurred. Rebuilt the whole anchor-detection mechanism
//           to make this possible: instead of fetching the anchor
//           timeframe's OHLC via request.security (a single value for the
//           whole period, with no visibility into which sub-bar printed
//           it, and lag-prone due to lookahead_off), the running high/low
//           is now tracked directly across the chart's own bars using
//           timeframe.change() — lag-free and bar-precise. Note the ray's
//           start position is now inherently timeframe-dependent: it will
//           point at a different bar depending on what timeframe you're
//           viewing, since that's the specific bar that prints the extreme.
//   3.2.0 - Fixed the ray starting one full anchor-period late (e.g. the
//           next day's candle instead of the actual anchor day) when viewed
//           on a lower timeframe than it was anchored on. Root cause:
//           request.security's lookahead_off setting (needed to avoid
//           repainting) only reveals a higher-timeframe value once that
//           period has fully closed, one period after it actually starts —
//           the code was using "the chart bar where this update became
//           visible" as the ray's start, which is that one-period-late bar.
//           Now looks up the actual chart bar carrying the anchor period's
//           real start timestamp instead. The price level itself was
//           already correct; only the starting position was off.
//   3.1.0 - Full auto-detection of "Anchor Candle's Timeframe" turns out to
//           be impossible: TradingView fully restarts the script (resetting
//           all runtime state) whenever the chart's timeframe changes, and
//           only explicitly-set inputs — never computed values — survive
//           that restart. As the next best thing, added an on-chart nudge
//           that appears right after anchoring if the input was left blank,
//           stating plainly that the ray is running on the ambiguous
//           "current chart" default and telling the user to set the input
//           explicitly before switching timeframes.
//   3.0.0 - Fixed incorrect price levels (and false breaks) when viewing the
//           chart on a different timeframe than the one the anchor was
//           picked on. Root cause: the clicked timestamp lands somewhere
//           inside the anchor candle's span, not necessarily its boundary
//           (e.g. 22:00 inside a Daily candle) — the old code then read
//           high/low from whatever bar of the *current* chart's timeframe
//           happened to contain that timestamp (e.g. one 4H slice of the
//           day), instead of the full candle the user actually selected.
//           Added an explicit "Anchor Candle's Timeframe" input and fetch
//           the true high/low for that period via request.security,
//           captured once the period closes — so the level is fixed and
//           correct no matter what timeframe you later view it on.
//   2.3.0 - Added an optional "Show Debug Info" label so a ray that appears
//           on one timeframe but not another can be diagnosed on-chart
//           instead of guessed at: it reports whether the anchor was found,
//           whether each ray has already broken, and the exact anchor vs.
//           current bar timestamps.
//   2.2.0 - Added an on-chart warning label for when the anchor candle isn't
//           reachable yet. Symptom: the ray was set on a higher timeframe
//           (e.g. Daily) and disappears after switching to a lower one
//           (e.g. 4H). Cause: TradingView lazy-loads chart history, and a
//           lower timeframe's initially-loaded window covers less calendar
//           time than a higher timeframe's — if the anchor predates that
//           window, the script hasn't seen a bar reaching it yet. Not
//           fixable from the script; the label tells the user to scroll/pan
//           left to load older history, instead of silently drawing nothing.
//   2.1.0 - Improved visibility on lower timeframes, where a thin line gets
//           lost among far more candles: bolder default color/width, an
//           optional soft "halo" line drawn behind the main line for
//           contrast, and an optional price label pinned to the latest bar
//           so the ray stays legible even after its anchor scrolls off-screen.
//   2.0.1 - Fixed rays drawing vertically instead of horizontally. The
//           initial line.new() call used the same bar_index for both x1 and
//           x2 (a zero-width line), which Pine resolves as vertical rather
//           than horizontal; x2 is now one bar to the right of the anchor.
//   2.0.0 - Rebuilt around a single anchor per instance (Anchored-VWAP-style
//           UX) instead of tracking many rays in one instance. Fixed anchor
//           detection to use a time-transition check
//           (time[1] < anchor <= time) instead of exact equality — exact
//           equality could silently never match a bar and draw nothing.
//   1.1.0 - Added manual candle-selection inputs alongside automatic
//           pivot-based rays.
//   1.0.0 - Initial release: automatic swing-high/low pivot rays, with
//           multi-timeframe pivot lookup.
// ============================================================================
indicator("Breaking Ray [vganesh] [v3.7.1]", overlay = true, max_lines_count = 500, max_labels_count = 50)

// ── Inputs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// confirm = true is what makes TradingView prompt a click-to-place UI, both
// on first adding the indicator and via the target icon in Settings later.
anchorTime = input.time(timestamp("2020-01-01T00:00"), "Anchor Candle", confirm = true, tooltip = "Click the candle you want the ray(s) to start from. To reposition it later, open Settings and click the target icon next to this field — no need to re-add the indicator. Add another instance of this indicator for each additional candle you want to track.")
anchorTF   = input.timeframe("", "Anchor Candle's Timeframe", tooltip = "Set this to the timeframe you were viewing when you clicked the Anchor Candle above (e.g. \"D\" for a Daily candle). This is what locks the ray to that candle's real high/low no matter what timeframe you view afterward. Blank = assume it's whatever timeframe the chart is on right now (only correct if you never change timeframes).")
rayMode    = input.string("Both", "Draw Ray From", options = ["Top", "Bottom", "Both"], tooltip = "Top = ray from the candle's high (resistance-style). Bottom = ray from the candle's low (support-style).")
breakBy    = input.string("Wick", "Break Detected By", options = ["Wick", "Close"], tooltip = "Wick = high/low touches the level. Close = candle must close through it.")
keepBroken = input.bool(true, "Keep Broken Rays As Lines", tooltip = "If off, a broken ray is deleted instead of left behind as a segment.")

rayColor    = input.color(color.new(#2962FF, 0), "Ray Color", group = "Style")
brokenColor = input.color(color.new(color.orange, 0), "Broken Line Color", group = "Style")
lineWidth   = input.int(2, "Line Width", minval = 1, maxval = 4, group = "Style")
styleInput  = input.string("Solid", "Line Style", options = ["Solid", "Dashed", "Dotted"], group = "Style")
showHalo    = input.bool(true, "Show Halo (Extra Visibility)", group = "Style", tooltip = "Draws a soft, wider line behind the ray so it stands out from candle wicks, especially on lower timeframes.")
showLabel   = input.bool(true, "Show Price Label", group = "Style", tooltip = "Pins a label with the ray's price to the latest bar, so it's easy to spot even when the anchor candle has scrolled off-screen.")
labelPos    = input.string("Left", "Price Label Position", options = ["Left", "Center", "Right"], group = "Style", tooltip = "Left = fixed at the anchor bar, never moves. Right = follows the ray's current right edge (the latest bar while active, the breaking bar once broken). Center = midpoint between the two — drifts right at half the rate of Right while the ray is active, then freezes once it breaks.")
showLabelAfterBreak = input.bool(false, "Show Price Label After Break", group = "Style", tooltip = "When a ray breaks and freezes into a line, the price label is dropped by default. Turn this on to keep showing it.")
showDebug   = input.bool(false, "Show Debug Info", group = "Style", tooltip = "Shows anchor/break state and timestamps on the latest bar — useful for diagnosing why a ray isn't appearing on a given timeframe.")

lineStyle(s) =>
    s == "Dashed" ? line.style_dashed : s == "Dotted" ? line.style_dotted : line.style_solid

// Where a ray's price label should sit given its fixed anchor bar and its
// current right edge (latest bar while active, the break bar once broken).
labelX(anchorBar, rightEdgeBar) =>
    switch labelPos
        "Left"   => anchorBar
        "Center" => int(math.round((anchorBar + rightEdgeBar) / 2))
        => rightEdgeBar

effTF = anchorTF == "" ? timeframe.period : anchorTF

// True on the chart's own first bar of each effTF period — lag-free, since
// it's computed directly from the chart's own bars rather than fetched
// from another context.
isNewPeriod = timeframe.change(effTF)

// ── State ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// var persists these across bars; each is set at most once (on the anchor
// bar) and then only read/mutated by the break check below.
var line  topLine      = na
var line  topHalo      = na
var label topLabel     = na
var float topLevel     = na
var bool  topBroken    = false
var int   topBreakBar  = na
var line  bottomLine   = na
var line  bottomHalo   = na
var label bottomLabel  = na
var float bottomLevel  = na
var bool  bottomBroken = false
var int   bottomBreakBar = na
var bool  anchored     = false
var label warnLabel    = na
var label debugLabel   = na
var label tfNudgeLabel = na

// Running high/low for whichever effTF period the chart is currently
// inside, plus the exact chart bar each one occurred on — rebuilt directly
// from the chart's own bars, bucketed by isNewPeriod.
var float curHigh        = na
var float curLow         = na
var int   curTopBar      = na
var int   curBotBar      = na
var int   curPeriodStart = na

// ── Anchor: rebuild the target period's high/low (and exact bars) directly ──
// from the chart's own data, instead of fetching the anchor timeframe via
// request.security. That approach needed lookahead_off to avoid repainting,
// which only reveals a higher-timeframe bar's data a full period after it
// closes — and it only ever gave a single high/low value for the whole
// period, with no way to know which lower-timeframe bar actually printed
// it. Tracking the running high/low across the chart's own bars gives both
// instantly, lag-free, and pinpoints the exact bar for each.
if isNewPeriod
    if not anchored and not na(curPeriodStart) and curPeriodStart <= anchorTime and anchorTime < time
        // The period that just closed (tracked in curHigh/curLow/curTopBar/
        // curBotBar) is the one containing anchorTime — finalize using it.
        anchored := true
        // x2 must differ from x1 (here, one bar to the right) — with x1 == x2,
        // Pine treats the zero-width line as vertical instead of horizontal,
        // and extend.right then extends it vertically instead of sideways.
        // The halo is created first so the sharp main line renders on top of it.
        if rayMode == "Top" or rayMode == "Both"
            topLevel := curHigh
            if showHalo
                topHalo := line.new(curTopBar, topLevel, curTopBar + 1, topLevel, extend = extend.right, color = color.new(rayColor, 70), width = lineWidth + 3, style = line.style_solid)
            topLine := line.new(curTopBar, topLevel, curTopBar + 1, topLevel, extend = extend.right, color = rayColor, width = lineWidth, style = lineStyle(styleInput))
        if rayMode == "Bottom" or rayMode == "Both"
            bottomLevel := curLow
            if showHalo
                bottomHalo := line.new(curBotBar, bottomLevel, curBotBar + 1, bottomLevel, extend = extend.right, color = color.new(rayColor, 70), width = lineWidth + 3, style = line.style_solid)
            bottomLine := line.new(curBotBar, bottomLevel, curBotBar + 1, bottomLevel, extend = extend.right, color = rayColor, width = lineWidth, style = lineStyle(styleInput))
    // Start tracking the new period, seeded with this bar's own values.
    curPeriodStart := time
    curHigh   := high
    curLow    := low
    curTopBar := bar_index
    curBotBar := bar_index
else if not anchored
    if high > curHigh
        curHigh   := high
        curTopBar := bar_index
    if low < curLow
        curLow    := low
        curBotBar := bar_index

// ── Break check: freeze a ray into a fixed segment the moment price breaks it ──
breakHighSrc = breakBy == "Wick" ? high : close
breakLowSrc  = breakBy == "Wick" ? low  : close

// Local (non-var) flag: recomputed every bar, true only on the bar a break happens,
// so alertcondition below fires once per event instead of staying true forever.
bool brokeThisBar = false

if not na(topLine) and not topBroken and breakHighSrc > topLevel
    topBroken := true
    topBreakBar := bar_index
    if keepBroken
        // extend.none + pinning x2 to the current bar turns the ray into a
        // plain segment from the anchor candle to the breaking candle.
        line.set_extend(topLine, extend.none)
        line.set_x2(topLine, bar_index)
        line.set_color(topLine, brokenColor)
        if not na(topHalo)
            line.set_extend(topHalo, extend.none)
            line.set_x2(topHalo, bar_index)
            line.set_color(topHalo, color.new(brokenColor, 70))
    else
        line.delete(topLine)
        line.delete(topHalo)
    alert("{{ticker}} Top ray broken at " + str.tostring(topLevel), alert.freq_once_per_bar)
    brokeThisBar := true

if not na(bottomLine) and not bottomBroken and breakLowSrc < bottomLevel
    bottomBroken := true
    bottomBreakBar := bar_index
    if keepBroken
        line.set_extend(bottomLine, extend.none)
        line.set_x2(bottomLine, bar_index)
        line.set_color(bottomLine, brokenColor)
        if not na(bottomHalo)
            line.set_extend(bottomHalo, extend.none)
            line.set_x2(bottomHalo, bar_index)
            line.set_color(bottomHalo, color.new(brokenColor, 70))
    else
        line.delete(bottomLine)
        line.delete(bottomHalo)
    alert("{{ticker}} Bottom ray broken at " + str.tostring(bottomLevel), alert.freq_once_per_bar)
    brokeThisBar := true

// ── Price label: repositioned every bar per labelPos while the ray is ──
// live, or (if kept and enabled) after it breaks. Fully state-driven — every
// bar either shows the label at the right spot or ensures it's gone, so
// break/keepBroken/showLabelAfterBreak transitions don't need special-casing
// elsewhere.
topLabelWanted = showLabel and not na(topLine) and (not topBroken or (keepBroken and showLabelAfterBreak))
label.delete(topLabel)
topLabel := na
if topLabelWanted
    topX = labelX(curTopBar, topBroken ? topBreakBar : bar_index)
    topLabel := label.new(topX, topLevel, "Top " + str.tostring(topLevel, format.mintick), style = label.style_label_left, color = topBroken ? brokenColor : rayColor, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

bottomLabelWanted = showLabel and not na(bottomLine) and (not bottomBroken or (keepBroken and showLabelAfterBreak))
label.delete(bottomLabel)
bottomLabel := na
if bottomLabelWanted
    bottomX = labelX(curBotBar, bottomBroken ? bottomBreakBar : bar_index)
    bottomLabel := label.new(bottomX, bottomLevel, "Bottom " + str.tostring(bottomLevel, format.mintick), style = label.style_label_left, color = bottomBroken ? brokenColor : rayColor, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

// ── Warn when the anchor candle hasn't been reached yet ────────────────
// Common cause: the anchor was picked on a higher timeframe (e.g. Daily),
// then the chart was switched to a lower one (e.g. 4H). TradingView lazily
// loads history, and a lower timeframe's initial window covers less
// calendar time than a higher one's — so the anchor date may simply not be
// loaded yet. Scrolling/panning left on the chart loads older bars and lets
// the script find it. Only drawn on the latest bar so it doesn't clutter history.
if barstate.islast
    label.delete(warnLabel)
    warnLabel := na
    if not anchored
        warnLabel := label.new(bar_index, high, "Breaking Ray [vganesh]: anchor candle not loaded yet.\nScroll/pan left to load older history.", style = label.style_label_down, color = color.new(color.red, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

// ── Nudge: flag reliance on the ambiguous "current chart" default ──────
// anchorTF == "" means effTF is recomputed as timeframe.period on every
// script run — correct only as long as you never view this ray on a
// different timeframe than the one you anchored it on. Since that's the
// exact mistake that caused the wrong-level bug fixed in 3.0.0, keep
// reminding the user to lock in an explicit value until they do.
if barstate.islast
    label.delete(tfNudgeLabel)
    tfNudgeLabel := na
    if anchored and anchorTF == ""
        tfNudgeLabel := label.new(bar_index, low, "Breaking Ray [vganesh]: 'Anchor Candle's Timeframe' is blank, so the level shown was computed using THIS chart's current timeframe (" + timeframe.period + ").\nThat's only correct if " + timeframe.period + " is the same timeframe you originally clicked the anchor candle on. If not, the level is wrong right now — go back to that original timeframe, note it, and set this input to it explicitly.", style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.yellow, 0), textcolor = color.black, size = size.small)

// ── Debug info: on-chart readout to diagnose "no ray" / "wrong level" reports ──
if showDebug and barstate.islast
    label.delete(debugLabel)
    debugText = "effTF=" + effTF +
                 "\nanchored=" + str.tostring(anchored) +
                 "\ntopLevel=" + str.tostring(topLevel) +
                 "\nbottomLevel=" + str.tostring(bottomLevel) +
                 "\ntopBroken=" + str.tostring(topBroken) +
                 "\nbottomBroken=" + str.tostring(bottomBroken) +
                 "\nanchorTime=" + str.format_time(anchorTime, "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm") +
                 "\ncurPeriodStart=" + (na(curPeriodStart) ? "n/a" : str.format_time(curPeriodStart, "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm")) +
                 "\nlastBarTime=" + str.format_time(time, "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm")
    debugLabel := label.new(bar_index, low, debugText, style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.gray, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

// alert() above fires with the dynamic "{{ticker}} Top/Bottom ray broken at
// <price>" message. alertcondition() below is a SEPARATE trigger exposed as
// its own named condition in the "Create Alert" dropdown — its message is
// fixed at compile time and can't carry which ray broke or at what price
// (brokeThisBar is just a combined yes/no flag), so this stays generic. For
// the detailed per-event message, create the alert with the condition set
// to "Any alert() function call" instead of "Ray Broken".
alertcondition(brokeThisBar, "Ray Broken", "{{ticker}} A breaking-ray level was broken.")
